    </div>

    <div class="definition-block">
      <p>Buying your first home in Australia with less than a 20% deposit? There is a good chance you will encounter Lenders Mortgage Insurance — a cost that surprises many first-time buyers because, despite its name, it does nothing to protect you. Understanding exactly what LMI is, how much it costs at different deposit levels, and the legitimate ways to avoid it can save you thousands of dollars before you even get the keys.</p>
    </div>


    <h2>What Is Lenders Mortgage Insurance?</h2>
    <p>Lenders Mortgage Insurance is a one-off premium that a borrower pays to protect the <em>lender</em> if the borrower defaults on a home loan. It kicks in whenever you borrow more than 80% of a property's value — in other words, when your deposit is less than 20%. The ratio of your loan to the property's value is called the Loan-to-Value Ratio, or LVR. Anything above 80% LVR is the trigger for LMI.</p>
    <p>It is worth pausing on that distinction: LMI protects the bank, not you. If you fall behind on repayments and the lender eventually sells the property at a loss, the LMI insurer covers the gap. You, as the borrower, are still liable for any remaining shortfall — and the insurer can pursue you to recover what it paid the bank. You are paying a premium for a policy that offers you zero direct benefit.</p>
    <p>In Australia, LMI is provided by two main insurers: <strong>Helia</strong> (formerly Genworth) and <strong>QBE</strong>. Some of the larger banks — Commonwealth Bank and Westpac among them — self-insure certain loan types, meaning they take on the risk themselves rather than using an external insurer. As a borrower, you generally cannot choose which insurer your lender uses; that arrangement is set at the lender level.</p>


    <h2>How Much Does LMI Cost in Australia?</h2>
    <p>LMI premiums are calculated as a percentage of the loan amount, not the property value. The rate itself is determined by two factors: your LVR and the size of your loan. Both push the premium higher as they increase, and the relationship is not linear — crossing certain LVR thresholds causes the rate to jump sharply.</p>
    <p>At an 85% LVR, a borrower might pay around 0.9% of the loan amount, while at 95% LVR that figure can reach 3.5% or more. Here are indicative ranges based on current market data:</p>

